From 7bb64fbeb5bc2e6335160122357fec7e4ec8c453 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: isyourbrainfoss <isyourbrainfoss@proton.me>
Date: Tue, 20 Oct 2026 23:38:21 +0200
Subject: [PATCH 2/7] firmware: qcom: serve the legacy QSEE RPMB listener for
 QTEE

The Fairphone 5 fingerprint trustlet keeps its storage key behind the
legacy QSEE RPMB listener (id 0x2000). While an smcinvoke call runs,
QTEE can return "blocked on listener" (result 1 or 2, 0xee02, listener
id). Mainline qcomtee has no handler for that, so the trustlet never
loads its saved templates.

This does what the out-of-tree qcomtee-bridge module did on 7.2.0:

- qcom_scm: add SMCs to register a QSEE listener (owner 50, service 2,
  command 1 or 6) and the QSEE log buffer (service 1, command 6). In
  both smcinvoke paths, when QTEE blocks on a listener, call the
  registered handler, then resume with the listener response (service
  2, command 3, args listener id and 0). Stop after 32 rounds.
- qcom_qsee_rpmb: at late init, take a buffer (0x6400, then 0x10000
  bytes) from its own tzmem pool and register listener 0x2000. Serve
  requests over the UFS RPMB well-known LU:
  0x104 reports device info and the write counter, 0x102 reads, and
  0x103 forwards the write frames the trustlet supplied (at most 8),
  then reads the result. 0x101 key provisioning is refused with -1,
  and key-program frames are never sent. Other commands get -5. The
  kernel never sends a write on its own.
- Register the QSEE log buffer (best effort) and expose it as
  /dev/qsee_log.

Built only with CONFIG_QCOMTEE. Needs SCSI to be reachable from it,
which it is in this config (both built in).
---
